WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:06.073
"Is Berberine “Nature’s Ozempic”?"

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.776
This review on
boosting the hormone GLP-1

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:10.911
by natural products

00:00:10.911 --> 00:00:12.913
concluded there are
compelling studies

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:16.083
that suggest that
the berberine in barberries,

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:18.819
quercetin in capers,
red onions, and buckwheat,

00:00:18.819 --> 00:00:21.889
ginseng, ginger root, gardenia,

00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:24.558
green tea,
wheat fiber, soybeans,

00:00:24.558 --> 00:00:26.526
curcumin in turmeric, cinnamon,

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:28.095
and resveratrol in grapes,

00:00:28.095 --> 00:00:31.465
each have potent effects
on GLP-1 activity.

00:00:31.465 --> 00:00:33.200
The hope is that
increasing the release

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.002
of our own GLP-1 hormone

00:00:35.002 --> 00:00:36.537
by eating specific foods

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:40.073
could have similar benefits
to GLP-1-mimicking drugs,

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:43.844
without their potentially
disabling side effects.

00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:46.580
But any time a review
makes a statement of fact,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.483
like, "Potent effects
on GLP-1 activity,"

00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:52.052
it’s always important
to dig up the original studies,

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.788
the so-called
"primary literature."

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:57.691
For example,
here’s the section on soy:

00:00:57.691 --> 00:00:59.493
researchers demonstrated
that soybeans

00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:00.761
can normalize blood sugars

00:01:00.761 --> 00:01:02.896
by inducing GLP-1 secretion,

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.765
dietary consumption
of soybean protein

00:01:04.765 --> 00:01:05.966
induces GLP-1

00:01:05.966 --> 00:01:07.167
and, moreover,

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.671
soybean protein increases
GLP-1 expression and secretion.

00:01:10.671 --> 00:01:12.806
"This combined evidence
strongly suggests

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.242
that soybean protein
exerts modulatory effects

00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:17.311
on GLP-1 secretion."

00:01:17.311 --> 00:01:18.645
Sounds great!

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:21.148
But if you pull
these three sources,

00:01:21.148 --> 00:01:24.251
that was based on studies
on mice, rats,

00:01:24.251 --> 00:01:26.720
and in Petri dishes.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.722
Gardenia-derived products

00:01:28.722 --> 00:01:31.491
also purportedly
potentiate GLP-1 secretion

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:33.694
based on cells and rodents,

00:01:33.694 --> 00:01:36.430
but, when actually tested
in humans,

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.931
those in the gardenia group

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:40.834
actually showed
a decrease in GLP-1.

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:44.338
Evidence also suggests ginseng
improves GLP-1 secretion,

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:45.439
but again, that’s just evidence

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:48.675
from rats and test tubes.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.611
What about that
red wine compound, resveratrol?

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.814
Works in diabetic rats,

00:01:53.814 --> 00:01:56.149
but not in diabetic humans.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:00.854
Green tea is said to increase
GLP-1 levels in actual patients,

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:03.156
actual human patients!

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:04.858
But, in fact,
the increase over placebo

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:08.395
did not reach
statistical significance.

00:02:08.395 --> 00:02:09.930
Olive oil is said to induce

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.333
higher GLP-1 concentrations
than butter,

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:16.103
but the study that was cited,
this one,

00:02:16.103 --> 00:02:19.573
actually showed
no significant difference.

00:02:19.573 --> 00:02:21.074
This was
on healthy subjects, though;

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:22.843
a similar study
on those with diabetes

00:02:22.843 --> 00:02:24.811
did show a larger
GLP-1 response,

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.214
though only by about 15 percent.

00:02:27.214 --> 00:02:30.384
Avocados have lots
of monounsaturated fats, too.

00:02:30.384 --> 00:02:32.686
And online,
you’ll find so-called experts

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:34.388
who claim that
eating avocados can be,

00:02:34.388 --> 00:02:37.391
"as effective as getting
weight-loss injections,"

00:02:37.391 --> 00:02:38.325
a food that works

00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:41.395
"just like the miracle
weight-loss jab Ozempic,"

00:02:41.395 --> 00:02:43.830
and that’s from a nutritionist!

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.466
"Guaca-mo-zempic"

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.835
may work if you’re a rat,

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.770
but in humans,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.272
GLP-1 was found to be
significantly lower

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:55.642
in the avocado-added meal.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.778
Speaking of online speculation,

00:02:57.778 --> 00:02:59.212
what do we know about berberine,

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.314
the supplement dubbed
"Nature’s Ozempic,"

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:02.682
a component of barberries,

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.451
a dried fruit
I’ve previously profiled

00:03:04.451 --> 00:03:06.353
as a treatment for acne?

00:03:06.353 --> 00:03:09.122
Well, berberine
boosts GLP-1 secretion

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.124
in rats and Petri dishes,

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:15.395
but there haven’t been any
GLP-1 studies on humans yet.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:16.963
What we care about, though,
is weight loss,

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:18.698
and there have been
a bunch of studies

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.434
on the effects
of berberine and barberries

00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:22.102
on body weight.

00:03:22.102 --> 00:03:23.870
No effect was found,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.238
either for berberine supplements

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.374
or barberries themselves.

00:03:28.442 --> 00:03:30.243
This is all very disappointing.

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:32.145
Something in the spice
"fenugreek"

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:34.548
seemed to boost GLP-1 signaling
in a test tube,

00:03:34.548 --> 00:03:37.284
but there are no human studies,

00:03:37.284 --> 00:03:38.952
and it didn’t even work in mice.

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:41.054
So, fenugreek flopped,

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:42.189
but there are some spices

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:45.192
that can legitimately
boost GLP-1 in humans,

00:03:45.192 --> 00:03:48.562
which I’ll cover next.

